# Binary files the leak gate cannot scan, reviewed by eye and recorded here.
#
# The gate refuses binaries by default because grep -I skips them, so it would
# otherwise report CLEAN on a file it never read -- which is exactly how a
# coverage database carrying absolute home-directory paths reached a public repo.
#
# FORMAT: one entry per line, `<sha256>  <path>` -- two spaces, and the path
# exactly as `git ls-files` prints it (no `./` prefix, no trailing whitespace).
# The comparison is a byte-exact whole-line match, so anything else is silently
# not an entry. The gate prints the exact line to paste when it refuses a file.
#
# The digest is the point, not decoration. A path-only entry would grant that
# path permanent immunity, so replacing `screenshot.png` with a re-capture that
# now shows a hostname in the URL bar would pass unreviewed. Keying on content
# means any replacement re-arms the gate and has to be reviewed again.
#
# Adding a line here is a statement that a human looked at THIS content. For a UI
# screenshot that means checking for a hostname or address in a URL bar, a
# connection panel, a tooltip or a breadcrumb.
#

